{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RP-06 chronology/failure checkpoint update (research-only; no tracked or production changes).\n\nAuthority correction: `crabbed-abstract-hapuka` (`761e-5af2-ed57-408a`) and commit `5a56202747` prove that today's unconditional, exactly-once Round-A fan-in for activated project LLM criteria was deliberate. However, the later 2026-08-16 RP-06 proposal immediately preceding the maintainer's `Approved` explicitly classified that behavior as an accidental semantic mismatch and assigned standard applicability plus inclusion/exclusion provenance to the shared Pass-1 framework (`.joe-janitor/raw-chat-2026-08-16.md:7980-8143`; recovered report `:438-476,657-683`). Therefore the approved end state supersedes the older behavior: an activated project criterion is evaluated against the effective applicability policy; if selected it runs exactly once in Round A, while disabled/non-applicable criteria run zero times with provenance. The implementation story must name this as an intentional reversal of 761e, not a compatibility-preserving refactor.\n\nNew executable defect: `.joe-janitor/rp06-failure-checkpoint-probe.py` injected one non-systemic ordinary Pass-1 criterion failure, then reran identical material with a healthy runner. First run: 1 failed model call, findings empty, `chunk_errors` absent, and checkpoint payload `[]`. Second run: `chunks_resumed=1`, healthy calls=0, findings empty. Root: `sizing.pass1_with_ladder` converts non-context exceptions to `([], calls)` before the outer failure counter; `_chunk` then persists that empty value as a completed checkpoint. A transient failure is consequently replayed as `succeeded(clean)` indefinitely until material changes.\n\nRequired shared-executor invariant: `failed` is distinct from `succeeded` with zero findings; failed/cancelled/shed units never write success checkpoints; consumed usage/failure classification survive; failed units retry next run. This changes replay/cost behavior explicitly but does not itself settle the current-run final-verdict policy.\n\nDecision-ready RP-06 queue is now: (D6.1) parity-first current-run failure projection versus posture-aware normalization; (D6.2) honor explicit code-review `usd_budget` during cutover versus journal-only shadowing first; (D6.3) preserve existing per-gate scheduler/cache modes during cutover versus activating code-review parallelism/checkpoints. Recommendation: typed/correct checkpoint outcomes immediately, parity-first gate verdicts and scheduling for the structural slice, explicit-budget enforcement because the authored request field should be truthful.\n\nPrior art independently rechecked at current upstream heads: ansible-lint `8824f98d49` shares typed rule selection/execution/results while rules own matching; Semgrep `808ee51cda` returns typed findings plus structured errors, then applies suppression and exit policy separately. Full note: `.joe-janitor/rp06-chronology-failure-critique.md`."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786914854166309001,"uuid":"e591becc-016f-4f22-a784-b6aaee6dd6c6"}